{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "הרם", :translit "harim", :gloss "lift up"} {:he "ג' במסו'", :translit "g' b'masu", :gloss "three in the Masorah"} {:he "הרם את מטך", :translit "harim et matekha", :gloss "lift up your staff"} {:he "כשופר הרם קולך", :translit "k'shofar harim kolekha", :gloss "like a shofar lift up your voice"} {:he "ויאמר הרם לך", :translit "vayomer harim lakh", :gloss "and he said lift up for yourself"} {:he "אידך", :translit "idakh", :gloss "the other one"} {:he "כשצף הברזל", :translit "k'shatsaf habarzel", :gloss "when the iron floated"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :masorah, :count 3, :occurrences ["הרם את מטך" "כשופר הרם קולך" "ויאמר הרם לך"]} {:kind :other, :note "Gezerah shavah linking the splitting of the sea and the floating of Elisha's iron axe-head on the shared word הרם, both miracles performed through water."}], :frame {:text "The floating of Elisha's iron axe-head is narrated at 2 Kings 6:5–7, where the prophet cuts a stick and throws it in, making the iron float. The command כשופר הרם קולך appears at Isaiah 58:1. The verse ויאמר הרם לך addressed to Elisha is at 2 Kings 6:6.", :sources ["2 Kings 6:5–7" "Isaiah 58:1" "2 Kings 6:6"]}} {:quotes [{:he "ובקעהו", :translit "uv'ka'ehu", :gloss "and split it"} {:he "ויבקע עצי עולה", :translit "vayvokea atzei olah", :gloss "and he split wood for a burnt offering"} {:he "בקע לגלגלת", :translit "baka lagulgolet", :gloss "he split the skull"} {:he "בקע", :translit "baka", :gloss "split"} {:he "וה\"ו", :translit "v'he'vav", :gloss "and the vav"} {:he "נבקעו המים", :translit "nivka'u hamayim", :gloss "the waters were split"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Gezerah shavah linking three instances of the root בקע — Abraham splitting wood for the burnt offering, the skull-crushing of Abimelech, and Moses splitting the sea — all in the merit of the patriarch."} {:kind :other, :note "Masoretic spelling observation: the vav-consecutive ויבקע versus the ordinary form בקע, with the vav mark